  "why": "The statement asks for the exact least x with π(x) > li(x). All upper and lower bounds on that quantity (Skewes through Saouter–Trudgian–Demichel, the computational lower bounds) predate the snapshot and are bounds; journal publication counts as digestion. Skewes 1933 is RH-conditional but still bounds the same quantity, so I scored it bound. Stoll–Demichel is heuristic, so none. The only in-window paper on the quantity, Revers 2025, sharpens the error terms and the count of positive integers inside regions already known, and lists unproven candidates. It moves neither bound on the least x, so it scores none. That was the close call. Platt–Trudgian (θ), Fiori–Kadiri–Swidinsky (|π−Li|), Bailleul et al. (prime races) and Anthropic's zeta-zeros result address different questions, so I scored them variant. Claude's generated role belongs to that variant event alone and does not carry over to this problem.",
